import{_ as t,o as r,c as s,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.DPTp4bo7.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-3e6Q3Y7LbtinO72F4eucBI.md"}'),i={name:"how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority.md"};function o(n,e,c,l,p,d){return r(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="优先汇入结构的末端箱子放置顺序影响优先级现象研究" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority" tabindex="-1">How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-terminal-stash-placement-order-affects-merge-priority" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>3e6Q3Y7LbtinO72F4eucBI</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=3e6Q3Y7LbtinO72F4eucBI" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>Ordinary merge priority, with no other splitter output, works as follows: when a splitter and belt (nonsplitter) directly connect to a converger, the earlier connection has priority; when nonsplitters have priority, multiple nonsplitters are serviced round-robin.</p><p>In this structure, modifying the downstream stash unpredictably changes the converger&#39;s priority, possibly due to component evaluation order.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-687f1d806e3643df.q90-015b4a3b4e20df54.webp" alt="How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority"></p><p>For now, experiments explained by jnk&#39;s layered BFS theory are labeled A; contrary results are B. Further classification or integration may be needed.</p><p>Recent experiments suggest the particular structure below follows these rules:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-961c72e9f7a3366f.png" alt="How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-c008e71f938e3bbd.q90-983b9f6c606432d9.webp" alt="How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority"></p><p>New results can be summarized empirically as follows:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-b2de1da8151cc5cb.q90-59f014433854cd20.webp" alt="How Terminal-Stash Placement Order Affects Merge Priority"></p><p>Treat the red-circled structure as a whole and calculate output damping. Splitting succeeds if the splitter has lower damping, analogous to higher priority, and the logistics component directly downstream of the converger was placed before the splitter. It also succeeds if the converger has lower damping and the splitter was placed before the component directly upstream of the converger. Otherwise it fails: the splitter outputs only away from the converger, potentially round-robin.</p><p>@madSUN&#39;s experimental results:</p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/attachments/test_priority_with_stash.txt-cd51ebe51ae5a869.txt">Attachment: test_priority_with_stash.txt</a></p><blockquote><p>Original: test_priority_with_stash.txt; size: 59412; objKey: <code>34fcf9d8c5534f31af9e62daba3e0d0a</code>; local status: downloaded.</p></blockquote><p>jnk explains these results as follows:</p><details><summary>Group chat: explanation</summary><p>jnk 2026/6/29 20:29:49<br> Conversely, if c-l precedes c-r, the splitter calculates damping first. It then depends on the connection order between the splitter and the converger&#39;s downstream component, with the splitter in control. Whichever direction was connected first calculates first; the converger&#39;s other upstream always calculates last. If the splitter connects first, evaluation order is “splitter input–output–belt input” [0]. Since splitter input is attempted before output, it cannot accept input and splitting fails; otherwise it succeeds. These three cases suggest that placing a converger connects upstream clockwise first, then downstream [1]:<br> &quot;c, c-l, c-r, s&quot;<br> &quot;c-l, c, c-r, s&quot;<br> &quot;c-l, c-r, c, s&quot;</p><p>jnk(QQ removed) 2026/6/29 20:29:57<br> Assuming [1], c-l must precede c-r for these reasons:</p><ol><li><p>With c last, it connects the upstream splitter before the downstream belt.</p></li><li><p>With c-r last, c and s are already placed and connected before connecting the downstream c-r belt.</p></li><li><p>c-l must precede c-r, so it cannot be last.<br> Thus s must end the sequence.</p></li></ol><p>jnk(QQ removed) 2026/6/29 20:30:11<br> The remaining nine cases:<br> l,r,s,c — converger placed last, connecting the splitter before the downstream belt.<br> l,s,r,c<br> s,l,r,c<br> l,s,c,r — downstream belt placed last, hence connected last.<br> l,c,s,r<br> c,l,s,r<br> s,l,c,r<br> c,s,l,r<br> s,c,l,r</p><p>jnk 2026/6/29 20:30:16<br> The input-before-output evaluation mentioned in [0] can, judging from the trigger conditions of the “4n, 1s stall,” trigger the “4n, 1s stall” here. The “4n, 1s stall” can be produced by keeping input from the other belt always empty while the Splitter&#39;s belt-direction output is blocked and cannot output.</p></details></div>',1)])])}const f=t(i,[["render",o]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
